    "description": "Iranian sweets include regional cookies and confections whose identities depend on ingredients, texture and preparation. Documented examples range from rice-flour cookies associated with Kermanshah to gaz, a nut-filled confection associated especially with Isfahan.",
    "tags": ["Sweets", "Cookies", "Confectionery", "Regional food", "Food gifts"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:23:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:23:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Sweets: Regional Cookies and Gaz",
      "description": "Discover documented Iranian cookie traditions, regional food gifts and the preparation of Isfahan's gaz."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-cookie-traditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A geography of cookies",
        "content": "Iranian cookies offer a useful introduction to the country's regional sweet-making. Encyclopaedia Iranica identifies rice-flour cookies, *nan-e berenji*, with Kermanshah while also documenting their reputation in Qazvin and Shiraz. Their name literally identifies rice bread, but here it denotes a delicate cookie. The documented dough combines rice flour, sugar, rose water, eggs and oil; shaping and surface impressions contribute to its appearance. [s1]\n\nOther cookies depend on different ingredients and techniques. Roasted chickpea flour gives *nan-e nokhodchi* its identity, while northern *kolucheh* can enclose a walnut-and-honey filling. Almond cookies associated with Tabriz belong to another part of this repertoire. These examples demonstrate regional associations without establishing exclusive ownership or a single recipe for each name. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gaz",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gaz and the confectioner's craft",
        "content": "Gaz is a confection associated especially with Isfahan. Iranica's account describes a traditional preparation using *gaz-angobin*, a sweet substance obtained from Khansar, together with egg whites, sugar, and pistachios or almonds. The sweet substance is dissolved and strained; beating, heating and the addition of nuts transform the mixture before it is shaped and cooled. [s2]\n\nTexture is central to its identity. The account describes a confection with both adhesive and brittle qualities, rather than a baked crumb. Pieces can be packed in loose flour to help prevent sticking and protect them from moisture. This also explains why flour surrounding a confection need not mean that it was baked as a flour-based cookie. [s2]\n\nThe source explicitly notes differences between confectioners, including closely guarded recipes passed between generations. Its description establishes a documented traditional method; it does not establish the ingredients of every contemporary product sold as gaz. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "occasions-and-gifts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making, sharing and taking home",
        "content": "Sweet-making connects domestic kitchens with specialist shops. Iranica documents cookies prepared for Nowruz, the Iranian New Year, alongside varieties made for other occasions. It also describes regional cookies given as travel gifts. In that setting, a sweet carries an association with a place as well as a particular flavor. This is a more precise way to understand the examples here than treating them as one interchangeable national dessert. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
