    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Tea culture in Iran connects brewing and serving practices with hospitality, workplaces and public meeting places. Its history also includes the development of domestic cultivation and the survival of the word coffeehouse for establishments that served tea.",
    "tags": ["Tea", "Hospitality", "Coffeehouses", "Food history", "Everyday culture"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:23:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:23:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Tea Culture in Iran: Brewing, Hospitality and History",
      "description": "Understand Iranian tea preparation, sugar on the side, historic coffeehouses and the development of domestic tea cultivation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "preparing-and-serving",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From concentrated brew to a glass of tea",
        "content": "Tea, *chay* in Persian, is both a beverage and a setting for social interaction. Iranica's historical account describes its presence in administrative offices and bazaar business, where serving tea accompanies work and conversation. These observations document established social habits rather than a rule followed by every Iranian. [s1]\n\nA characteristic preparation uses black tea brewed with a little water in a small teapot, or *quri*, kept above a kettle or samovar. Concentrated tea is poured into a glass and diluted with hot water. The drinker can therefore adjust its strength: *por-rang* describes a darker, stronger glass, while *kam-rang* describes a lighter one. The familiar glass, *estekan*, is served on a saucer. [s1]\n\nSugar need not be stirred into the liquid. In the practice called *qand-pahlu*, sugar is served alongside the tea and may be held in the mouth while drinking. The same account records regional variations, including citrus additions and milk tea among communities in the southeast. These examples should be read as documented variation, not as a complete map of present-day preferences. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cultivation-and-trade",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Drinking tea and growing tea",
        "content": "The history of tea drinking should be distinguished from the history of cultivating the crop inside Iran. Mohammad Mirza Kashef-al-Saltana, a diplomat and agricultural reformer, played a prominent role in promoting domestic cultivation around the turn of the twentieth century. His interest developed during service in India, where he studied agricultural practices and sent tea seeds and cultivation literature to Tehran. He also brought plants back to Iran. [s2]\n\nThe project had an economic purpose as well as a botanical one. Kashef-al-Saltana and government officials viewed domestic tea cultivation as a way to meet demand and reduce reliance on imports. His expectation that Iran could eventually become self-sufficient was an aspiration, not proof that self-sufficiency followed. The distinction matters: a successful effort to establish cultivation does not by itself demonstrate that domestic harvests subsequently supplied all tea consumed in the country. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coffeehouse-name",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why a tea-serving place might be called a coffeehouse",
        "content": "The word *qahveh-khaneh* means coffeehouse. It remained in use after tea displaced coffee as the principal beverage in these establishments; *chay-khaneh*, or teahouse, also circulated. A historical name can therefore preserve an earlier drinking culture even when the contents of the cup have changed. [s3]\n\nThese venues were also meeting places. Accounts of Safavid coffeehouses describe poets, artists and other visitors gathering there, while later establishments hosted recitations and storytelling. Their significance lay partly in the social activity around the beverage. A teapot alone cannot explain the institution: conversation, performance and the availability of a shared meeting space were integral to its history. [s3]\n\nHistorical descriptions nevertheless require care. The encyclopedia account discusses particular periods and establishments, including spaces strongly associated with male sociability. It does not establish the clientele or atmosphere of every contemporary café. Tea culture is best understood through several connected histories: preparing a drink, welcoming company, operating public meeting places and developing a domestic crop. Each illuminates a different part of the subject. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
